              "kind": "suspected_slowdown",
              "family": "Unpickling a legacy ``__dict__`` that contains exactly the five slot keys.",
              "why": "Old code did ``self.__dict__.update(state)`` (single C call). New code checks ``state.keys() >= set(self.__slots__)`` then loops over the five slot names doing ``setattr`` – extra Python‑level work for every unpickle.",
              "size_meaning": "Number of extra dummy keys added to the dict (the dict always contains the five real keys plus *n* harmless extra keys).",
              "make_source": "def make(n):\n    # Build a list of state dicts that mimic the pre‑26.1 pickle format.\n    # Each dict has the five required keys plus *n* additional keys.\n    base = {\n        \"name\": \"demo\",\n        \"url\": None,\n        \"extras\": {\"dev\"},\n        \"specifier\": \"==1.0\",\n        \"marker\": None,\n    }\n    out = []\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        d = dict(base)\n        for k in range(i):\n            d[f\"_junk{k}\"] = k\n        out.append([d])\n    return out",
